---
import GenericPage from '../components/GenericPage.astro';
import NavBar from '../components/NavBar.astro';
import { fetchPredictions } from '../utils/predictions.js';

const baseUrl = /** @type {string} */ ((import.meta as any).env?.BASE_URL) || '/';
const predictions = fetchPredictions();

const methodology = predictions?.methodology ?? null;
const ex = methodology?.example ?? null;
const scoredWeeks = methodology?.scored_weeks ?? null;
const weekLabel = predictions?.predictedWeek?.label ?? null;

const round1 = (v: number | null | undefined) =>
	v == null ? null : Math.round(v * 10) / 10;
// slot_hit_rate is 0-1; likelihood_pct is already 0-100.
const slotRatePct = ex ? round1(ex.slot_hit_rate * 100) : null;
---

<GenericPage
	title="Methodology - WRF Discounts"
	description="How the WRFrontiers discount predictions are calculated: ranking by weeks-since-discount, then position-calibrated odds from a no-look-ahead backtest."
	baseUrl={baseUrl}
>
	<NavBar baseUrl={baseUrl} currentPage="predictions" />
	<h1>How predictions are calculated</h1>
	<div class="method-content">
		<p class="intro">
			The <a href={`${baseUrl}predictions`}>Predictions</a> page estimates which
			robots and titans are most likely to be discounted next. Nothing here is an
			official announcement &mdash; it is a statistical read of past discount
			patterns. This page lays out exactly how the numbers are produced, in two
			steps: first an <strong>ordering</strong>, then a <strong>likelihood</strong>
			for each position in that ordering.
		</p>

		<section>
			<h2>Step 1 &mdash; Rank by how overdue each robot is</h2>
			<p>
				Every robot that has been discounted before has an average cadence. The
				ranking signal is simply <strong>weeks since it was last discounted</strong>:
				the most overdue robot ranks first, the next most overdue second, and so on.
				This plain "most overdue wins" rule was the most accurate ordering we
				tested &mdash; notably, it beat dividing the wait by each robot's average
				interval, so the raw wait is what we use.
			</p>
			<p class="fine">
				A robot needs at least <strong>2 prior discounts</strong> to be ranked. A
				robot discounted zero or one times has no established cadence yet, so it is
				left out of the ranking and out of the accuracy math alike. In practice
				these are usually the <strong>newest robots</strong>.
			</p>
			<p class="fine">
				One honest consequence: an excluded robot can still take a discount slot
				&mdash; a brand-new robot getting its long-awaited first or second discount
				&mdash; and when it does, it displaces one of the robots we listed. Because
				that robot has no cadence to measure, we cannot fold its long wait into the
				odds in any accurate way, so we leave it out entirely. The upshot is that
				the real chance for each listed robot is <strong>slightly lower</strong>
				than the percentage shown, by however often a newcomer claims a slot.
			</p>
		</section>

		<section>
			<h2>Step 2 &mdash; Turn each rank position into a likelihood</h2>
			<p>
				The percentage shown is <strong>position-calibrated</strong>. We replay
				history week by week &mdash; each week using <em>only</em> the discounts
				before it, so the model never sees the answer it is predicting &mdash; and
				measure how often the robot sitting in each rank slot was actually
				discounted. That measured hit-rate becomes the likelihood for whoever lands
				in that slot next.
			</p>
			<div class="formula">
				likelihood(slot) = weeks the slot was actually discounted &divide; weeks scored
			</div>
			<p>
				Because it is measured directly, the likelihood cannot drift from reality:
				it <em>is</em> the observed frequency. The backtest reruns on every build,
				so as new weeks are archived the numbers recalibrate themselves.
			</p>
			<p class="fine">
				The cards are then sorted by this likelihood, not by the raw wait. That is
				deliberate: a very long dry spell often belongs to a robot that keeps being
				skipped, so being the <em>most</em> overdue is not the same as being the
				<em>most likely</em>. The worked example below shows exactly this.
			</p>
		</section>

		{ex && (
			<section class="example">
				<h2>Worked example{weekLabel ? <> &mdash; {weekLabel}</> : null}</h2>
				<p class="disclaimer">
					These figures are this week's actual numbers, shown only to make the
					method concrete. <strong>They change every week</strong> as the history
					grows and the predicted week moves &mdash; do not read them as fixed
					constants.
				</p>
				<ol class="walk">
					<li>
						<strong>{ex.name}</strong> was last discounted{' '}
						<strong>{ex.weeks_since_discount}</strong> weeks ago, which makes it the{' '}
						<strong>#{ex.overdue_rank}</strong> most overdue eligible robot this
						week (Step&nbsp;1).
					</li>
					<li>
						Across <strong>{scoredWeeks}</strong> backtested weeks, the robot in
						rank slot <strong>#{ex.overdue_rank}</strong> was actually discounted{' '}
						<strong>{ex.slot_hits}</strong> times (Step&nbsp;2).
					</li>
					<li>
						<div class="formula formula--calc">
							{ex.slot_hits} &divide; {scoredWeeks} = {slotRatePct}%
						</div>
						So <strong>{ex.name}</strong> is shown at{' '}
						<strong>{round1(ex.likelihood_pct)}%</strong> &mdash; the number on its
						card on the Predictions page.
					</li>
				</ol>
				<p class="fine">
					Note that the rank&nbsp;#{ex.overdue_rank} slot can out-score the
					rank&nbsp;#1 slot &mdash; the most overdue robot is not always the most
					likely, which is why the list is sorted by likelihood.
				</p>
			</section>
		)}

		<section>
			<h2>Titans</h2>
			<p>
				Titans are discounted in only a minority of weeks, so their odds are framed
				conditionally: "if a titan is discounted next, the odds it is this one." The
				Predictions page also notes how often no titan is discounted at all, since
				the next titan discount may not fall next week.
			</p>
		</section>

		<section>
			<h2>Honesty &amp; track record</h2>
			<p>
				Every past week's prediction is frozen at the time it was made and graded
				against what was actually discounted &mdash; see the{' '}
				<a href={`${baseUrl}history`}>Prediction History</a> page for the week-by-week
				record and a rolling accuracy scoreboard. The live calibration trend is also
				kept as raw data in <code>accuracy_history.json</code>, so anyone can audit
				how the numbers have held up over time.
			</p>
		</section>

		<p class="back">
			<a href={`${baseUrl}predictions`}>&larr; Back to predictions</a>
		</p>
	</div>
</GenericPage>

<style>
	.method-content {
		max-width: 820px;
		margin: 0 auto;
		line-height: 1.75;
	}

	.intro {
		color: var(--text-color);
		margin-bottom: 2rem;
	}

	section {
		margin-bottom: 2.25rem;
		padding-bottom: 1.5rem;
		border-bottom: 1px solid var(--sidebar-border);
	}

	section:last-of-type {
		border-bottom: none;
	}

	h2 {
		color: var(--accent-color);
		margin-bottom: 0.85rem;
	}

	p {
		margin-bottom: 1rem;
		color: var(--text-color);
	}

	.fine {
		color: var(--text-muted);
		font-size: 0.9rem;
	}

	a {
		color: var(--accent-color);
		text-decoration: none;
	}

	a:hover {
		text-decoration: underline;
	}

	.formula {
		font-family: 'Courier New', ui-monospace, monospace;
		background: var(--sidebar-bg);
		border: 1px solid var(--sidebar-border);
		border-radius: 8px;
		padding: 0.85rem 1rem;
		margin: 1rem 0;
		color: var(--text-color);
		overflow-x: auto;
		white-space: nowrap;
	}

	.formula--calc {
		font-size: 1.15rem;
		color: var(--accent-color);
	}

	.example {
		background: var(--active-bg);
		border: 1px solid var(--sidebar-border);
		border-radius: 12px;
		padding: 1.25rem 1.5rem;
	}

	.disclaimer {
		color: var(--text-muted);
		font-size: 0.9rem;
		font-style: italic;
	}

	.walk {
		margin: 1rem 0;
		padding-left: 1.25rem;
	}

	.walk li {
		margin-bottom: 0.9rem;
		color: var(--text-color);
	}

	code {
		font-family: 'Courier New', ui-monospace, monospace;
		background: var(--sidebar-bg);
		border: 1px solid var(--sidebar-border);
		border-radius: 4px;
		padding: 0.1rem 0.35rem;
		font-size: 0.85em;
	}

	.back {
		margin-top: 2rem;
		text-align: center;
	}

	@media (max-width: 768px) {
		.method-content {
			padding: 0 0.5rem;
		}
	}
</style>
